::PC_U05_L06_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U05_L06_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.6
Question:
What is the center of (x - 2)^2/16 + (y + 1)^2/9 = 1?
{
~A. (-2, 1)#This reverses both signs.
=B. (2, -1)#Correct. The center is (h, k) = (2, -1).
~C. (2, 1)#This misses the y-coordinate sign.
~D. (-2, -1)#This misses the x-coordinate sign.
}
::PC_U05_L06_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U05_L06_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.7
Question:
For x^2/25 + y^2/9 = 1, which axis is the major axis?
{
~A. Vertical#The larger denominator is under x^2, so the major axis is horizontal.
~B. Neither axis#There is a larger denominator, so there is a major axis.
~C. Both axes equally#Equal denominators would describe a circle, not this ellipse.
=D. Horizontal#Correct. The larger denominator under x^2 means the major axis is horizontal.
}
::PC_U05_L06_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U05_L06_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.6
Question:
For x^2/36 + y^2/16 = 1, what is the semi-major axis length?
{
=A. 6#Correct. The larger denominator is 36, so the semi-major axis length is sqrt(36) = 6.
~B. 36#This gives the squared value, not the length.
~C. 4#This is the semi-minor axis length.
~D. 16#This is the smaller denominator, not its square root.
}
::PC_U05_L06_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U05_L06_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.7
Question:
Which real-world situation is often modeled by an ellipse?
{
~A. A straight road#A straight road is linear, not elliptical.
~B. A perfect right angle#A right angle is not an ellipse.
=C. An orbit path#Correct. Orbit paths are often modeled using ellipses.
~D. A single point#A single point does not show ellipse structure.
}
::PC_U05_L06_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U05_L06_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.PA.6; MLA.PC.PA.7
Question:
Why is the larger denominator important in an ellipse equation?
{
~A. It is ignored when graphing.#The denominator is essential for graphing.
=B. It identifies the major-axis direction and length.#Correct. The larger denominator shows the major-axis direction and its square root gives the semi-major axis length.
~C. It always gives the center.#The center comes from h and k shifts.
~D. It means the graph is a parabola.#A standard ellipse equation still graphs an ellipse.
}